// draft
// drafted
type ExtractError = { code: Text, display: Text }

// drafted
index_beyond_error: ExtractError = {code: "IndexError", display: "An index lies beyond the iterable"}
// drafted
negative_index_error: ExtractError = {code: "ValueError", display: "An index is negative"}
// drafted
decreasing_index_error: ExtractError = {code: "ValueError", display: "An index is less than the one before it while monotonic is true"}

extract(iterable: Text[], indices: Int[], monotonic: Bool) -> Result<Text[], ExtractError>
	note "yield the iterable's values at the given indices, in the order the indices are given, with repeats allowed"
	# The output is Ok exactly when every index is at least 0 and less than the iterable's length,
	# and, when monotonic is true, no index is less than the index before it.
	// reaching for: one line joining two quantified operands with <->, "(all i in indices: i >= 0
	// and i < len(iterable) and all j in indices where monotonic and i before j in indices: i <= j)
	// <-> exactly_one Ok(_) in out". Comparing each index with every earlier one says the same as
	// comparing it with the one just before it. A quantifier has to be the last operand on a line,
	// so this needs the condition as an obligation-level abbreviation, like apply_diff's `checks`,
	// and that abbreviation is the author's to name.
	# An Ok output has as many elements as there are indices.
	all Ok(res) in out: len(res) == len(indices)
	# For every position in indices, the Ok output's element at that position is the iterable's
	# element at the index found at that position of indices.
	// reaching for: a binder over the positions of a list, so the line could say "for every
	// position k of indices, res[k .. k + 1] == iterable[i .. i + 1], where i is the element of
	// indices at position k". Binders range over occurrences but never expose their position as an
	// Int, and nothing pairs the k-th element of one list with the k-th element of another.
	# When monotonic is false and some index is less than 0, the error is the negative-index error,
	# whatever else is wrong with the indices.
	all i in indices where not monotonic and i < 0: all Err(e) in out: e == negative_index_error
	# When monotonic is false, the error is the beyond-the-iterable error exactly when every index
	# is at least 0 and some index is at least the iterable's length.
	all Err(e) in out where not monotonic: e == index_beyond_error <-> (any j in indices: j >= len(iterable) and (all i in indices: i >= 0))
	# When monotonic is true, the error reported is the one for the first index in indices that is
	# negative, at least the iterable's length, or less than the index before it.
	all Err(e) in out where monotonic: any f in indices: any p in indices: (f < 0 and e == negative_index_error or f >= len(iterable) and e == index_beyond_error or p before f in indices and f < p and e == decreasing_index_error) and (all g in indices where g before f in indices: 0 <= g and g < len(iterable) and (all q in indices where q before g in indices: q <= g))
	// example wanted: the docstring's alphabet with indices [7, 4, 11, 11, 14], monotonic false:
	// the indices are out of order and repeated, and neither is an error when monotonic is false
	// example wanted: empty indices over a non-empty iterable, and empty indices over an empty
	// iterable: no index means nothing is checked
	// example wanted: an empty iterable with indices [0]: index 0 is already beyond an empty
	// iterable
	// example wanted: a three-element iterable with indices [2], then with indices [3]: the exact
	// threshold, last position against one past the end
	// example wanted: indices [-1] with monotonic false: a negative index never counts from the end
	// example wanted: monotonic true with indices [1, 1, 2]: equal neighbours are non-decreasing,
	// so no decreasing error
	// example wanted: monotonic true with indices [2, 1]: a decrease that is reported only because
	// monotonic is true
	// example wanted: monotonic false with indices [5, -1] over a three-element iterable: the
	// beyond index comes first but the negative check decides
	// example wanted: monotonic true with indices [5, 2] over a three-element iterable: beyond the
	// iterable and then a decrease, and the earlier index decides
	// example wanted: monotonic true with indices [1, -1]: the second index is both negative and a
	// decrease, so the implementation's check order decides which ValueError is reported
